# Design principles

Five rules govern every component in this library and every page built
from it. When a build decision is unclear, these settle it — in this
order.

## Native first

Reach for what the browser ships before writing JavaScript.

- Disclosure is `<details>`/`<summary>` — the accordion is zero-JS,
  and single-open behaviour comes from the native shared `name`
  attribute, not a controller.
- Blocking overlays are native `<dialog>` with `showModal()` — focus
  trapping, the Escape key and the backdrop come free. The sheet and
  dialog share one small Stimulus controller for open/close wiring
  only.
- Scrolling regions are `overflow-y: auto`, not a custom-scrollbar
  component.
- Form controls are native `<input>` elements styled by basecoat —
  checkbox, radio, switch are all `type="checkbox|radio"` underneath.

If a behaviour seems to need new JavaScript, first check whether an
HTML element, attribute, or CSS feature covers it.

## Basecoat before custom CSS

Basecoat ships the visual system: `btn` and its variant suffixes,
`.table`, `.input`, `.dialog`, `.select`, `.command`, `.toast`. Use the
shipped class before writing any CSS. A component only gets a file in
`app/assets/tailwind/components/` for styling basecoat does not
provide — and that file extends the basecoat treatment rather than
replacing it. Page-level spacing and layout use Tailwind utilities;
component look-and-feel never does.

## No new dependencies

The runtime dependency set is closed: Rails, Turbo, Stimulus,
basecoat (CSS + JS from the `basecoat-css` npm package), Tailwind. Component
behaviour that basecoat's bundle already implements — select,
command, toast — is wired, not rewritten. Anything that appears to
need a new gem or npm package (charts, rich date pickers) is decided
per consuming feature, never added to the library speculatively.

## Build the minimum

A component earns its place by having a consuming page, not by
existing in some other design system. Variants, params and options are
added when a real page needs them. The partials take the smallest
local set that covers current use — a handed-in pagination window
instead of page maths, a flash-to-toast mapping instead of a
programmatic toast API. When in doubt, leave it out; gaps are cheap to
fill and dead options are expensive to carry.

Start inline. New UI is written directly in the page that needs it,
not pre-extracted into a partial on the chance it recurs. It graduates
to a component on the **third** instance of the same thing — two
copies are a coincidence, three are a pattern. Inlining first and
refactoring later is cheaper than carrying an abstraction no second
page ever wanted.

A component bundles UI/UX **and** behaviour: the markup, its basecoat
styling, and the state, logic or accessibility contract that travels
with it. That bundle is what earns the extraction — not the markup
alone. So one trigger is duplication (the third instance); the other
is responsibility. A piece that carries behaviour or an accessibility
contract a caller would otherwise get wrong — a focus-managed dialog,
a keyboard-operable combobox — becomes a component on its *first* use,
because inlining it once is how the bug ships. A single styled element
with a class is neither: the class is already the API, so it stays
inline.

## Accessible by default

Accessibility lives inside the component so callers cannot forget it:
`role="progressbar"` on progress, `role="separator"` on separators,
`aria-label` on pagination's `<nav>`, `role="listbox"`/`combobox` in
the select pattern, `aria-selected` marking the chosen option. Native
elements (see above) carry their own keyboard handling. A new
component is not done until it is operable by keyboard alone and
announces its state — that ships in the first commit, not as a
follow-up.
